b/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287712 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "614d2565-08b2-5cdc-9df9-02ccb7032ebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287712 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nB={-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}$ given below.\n\n${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n${z}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1cb23c2-1fc6-50c0-a109-f29b4b4fe8eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287732 #edb/ref 1074 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some cubic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the cubic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=2,$ ${z}_{2}=3-\\text{i},$ and ${z}_{3}=3+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2})(z-{z}_{3}) \\\\  & =(1)(z-2)(z-(3-\\text{i}))(z-(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-z(3+\\text{i})-z(3-\\text{i})+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-6z+10) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-6{z}^{2}+10z-2{z}^{2}+12z-20 \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as\n\n$$\n{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20=0}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8be62d80165b756aedf31e998d6636d7f77a5606e195b3ec288cb0ac6fb52d6f.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "706b2b43-7315-5cd0-9807-cc687006ade4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287769 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,4).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+16(4)^{2}\\,>\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $>$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288137 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in (0,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288137 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288137 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ef6f08b-f29d-5594-8f2c-27a0f21f015d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288430 #edb/ref 1080 "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288430 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288430 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a02222e-7bac-556f-8a59-fb96cc7379fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
